1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,328
Hello! Hangeul, the most scientific writing system in the world.

2
00:00:05,328 --> 00:00:13,980
Today, we'll uncover how Hangeul was born and the secrets of its eight single vowels.

3
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:20,842
Long, long ago, we didn't have our own writing system, so we borrowed difficult Chinese characters.

4
00:00:20,842 --> 00:00:25,740
Chinese characters were too complex, and many common people couldn't read.

5
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:32,986
King Sejong, seeing his people suffer injustice due to illiteracy, deeply empathized with their plight.

6
00:00:32,986 --> 00:00:38,010
'I pity them greatly, and thus create twenty-eight new letters.'

7
00:00:38,710 --> 00:00:45,806
Finally, in 1443, Hunminjeongeum, a script easy for everyone to learn, was born.

8
00:00:45,806 --> 00:00:51,690
The wise could master it in a morning, and anyone could learn it in ten days.

9
00:00:52,390 --> 00:00:59,340
Hangeul vowels were created by modeling the three elements of the universe: heaven, earth, and humanity.

10
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:06,290
Heaven is a round dot, earth is a flat horizontal line, and humanity is a straight vertical line.

11
00:01:06,990 --> 00:01:12,315
When the dot of heaven is placed to the right of the vertical line representing humanity,

12
00:01:12,315 --> 00:01:16,171
like the rising sun in the east — a bright sound 'ㅏ'!

13
00:01:16,171 --> 00:01:21,680
If it's placed to the left, like the setting sun in the west — a dark sound 'ㅓ'!

14
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:29,430
When the dot is above the horizontal line representing earth, it's 'ㅗ', and when it's below, it's 'ㅜ'.

15
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:34,310
Rising upwards is bright, sinking downwards is dark.

16
00:01:35,010 --> 00:01:43,340
Thus, bright vowels harmonize with bright vowels, and dark vowels with dark vowels.

17
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:47,990
This beautiful order is called 'vowel harmony.'

18
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:54,821
Shall we try making the sounds now? There are eight Korean single vowels.

19
00:01:54,821 --> 00:02:04,585
Unlike English, the key is that your mouth shape should not waver while making the sound.

20
00:02:04,585 --> 00:02:12,760
Let's practice together: ㅏ, ㅓ, ㅗ, ㅜ, ㅡ, ㅣ, ㅐ, ㅔ.

21
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:16,074
First, 'ㅏ' and 'ㅓ'.

22
00:02:16,074 --> 00:02:25,440
For both, drop your jaw wide open vertically and keep your lips flat.

23
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:30,450
'ㅏ' is wider, 'ㅓ' is slightly narrower.

24
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:39,501
For 'ㅗ' and 'ㅜ', round your lips as if holding a straw and push them forward.

25
00:02:39,501 --> 00:02:45,000
'ㅗ' is a bit larger, 'ㅜ' is smaller and more pointed.

26
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:50,090
For 'ㅡ', flatten your lips sideways like an forced smile,

27
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:55,780
and for 'ㅣ', stretch them even wider so your teeth are slightly visible.

28
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:04,705
Finally, 'ㅐ' and 'ㅔ'. Open your mouth moderately and sound them with your tongue placed forward.

29
00:03:04,705 --> 00:03:08,380
Today, these two sounds are pronounced almost identically.

30
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,700
Check in the mirror!

31
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:22,400
Only when saying 'ㅗ, ㅜ' should your lips be rounded; never for 'ㅓ, ㅡ'.

32
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:27,907
Practice the single vowels you learned today every day in front of a mirror.

33
00:03:27,907 --> 00:03:35,200
Subscribe and like for more exciting Hangeul journeys. See you next time!
